# A Trivalent Americium Organic Framework with Decent Structural Stability against Self‐Irradiation

## abstract
Comprehensive Summary: We reported the synthesis, single crystal structure, and solid‐state UV‐Vis‐NIR spectroscopy of a new transplutonium metal‐organic framework (MOFs), Am(H2O)[PO[(C6H4)COO]3], denoted as AmTPO (TPO = tris(4‐carboxylphenyl)‐phosphineoxide). AmTPO forms a three‐dimensional metal−organic framework structure with americium dimers as the secondary building unit. Clear 5f → 5f transitions attributed to trivalent americium was observed in the absorption spectrum of AmTPO ranging from 300 to 1200 nm. Notably, AmTPO can maintain the crystallinity with no observable structural degradation within several months after being synthesized, revealing a long‐term radiation resistance of this structure and the potential application of MOFs as a platform for nuclear waste disposal.

State Key Laboratory of Radiation Medicine and Protection, School for Radiological and Interdisciplinary Sciences (RAD-X) and Collaborative Innovation Center of Radiation Medicine of Jiangsu Higher Education Institutions, Soochow University, Soochow, Jiangsu 215123, China
## Comprehensive Summary
We reported the synthesis, single crystal structure, and solid-state UV-Vis-NIR spectroscopy of a new transplutonium metal-organic framework (MOFs), $\mathsf{A m}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})[\mathsf{P O}[(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{4})\mathsf{C O O}]_{3}],$ denoted as AmTPO ( ${\mathsf{T P O}}=$ tris(4-carboxylphenyl)-phosphineoxide). AmTPO forms a three-dimensional metal−organic framework structure with americium dimers as the secondary building unit. Clear $5f\rightarrow5f$ transitions attributed to trivalent americium was observed in the absorption spectrum of AmTPO ranging from 300 to $1200\mathsf{n m}$ . Notably, AmTPO can maintain the crystallinity with no observable structural degradation within several months after being synthesized, revealing a long-term radiation resistance of this structure and the potential application of MOFs as a platform for nuclear waste disposal.
Keywords
Metal-organic Frameworks | Transplutonium | Americium | Radiochemistry | Actinides
## Background and Originality Content
As the first transplutonium element, the minor actinide element 95, americium, was first produced by Seaborg et al. during 1944—1945 by neutron bombardment of $^{\dot{2}39}\mathsf{P u}$ .[1] $^{2\breve{4}1}{\mathsf{A m}}$ (half-life $(t_{1/2})=432$ years) and its long-lived isotope $^{243}{\mathsf{A m}}$ (half-life $(t_{1/2})=$ 7370 years) could be also generated through the cycle of neutron bombardment and beta-decay of actinides in the nuclear flue of reactors. Furthermore, the long-lived isotopes of Am, 241Am and 243Am, can be transmuted into the radionuclides with much shorter-half life, which are critical for the end of nuclear fuel cycle.[2] However, the separation of $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ from other fission products is a key process to this goal and remains a great challenge because of the similar coordination chemistry between $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ and the other trivalent lanthanides.[3] Therefore, comprehensively understanding the coordination chemistry of Am-based structure may contribute to exploring new approach for separation of $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ in nuclear fuel cycle and also paving the way to develop new actinidebased functional materials.[4]
Different from the early-actinide elements (U, Th, Np, Pu), trivalent oxidation state is the most stable and common oxidation state for americium under standard condition in part because of the lowering energy of the 5f orbital and special reduction potentials, although the penta- and hexavalent oxidation states also exist as the linear dioxo ${\mathsf{A}}{\mathsf{m}}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}^{\ +}$ and $\mathsf{A m O}_{2}^{2+}$ as ‘americyl’ units.[5] Due to the strong radioactivity and high toxicity, limited americium-based structures were reported when comparing to those of the main group, transition metals, and lanthanides. Previous studies about the structure of $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ have been focused on the common inorganic materials, such as phosphite,[6] iodate,[7] tungstate,[8] among other. The term ‘Metal-Organic Framework (MOF)’ has been used to describe an expansive class of three-dimensional hybrid materials, which consist of the metal center as the node and organic ligand as the linker.[9] Importantly, the variable structures of MOFs via changing ligands provide an opportunity to exploit subtle differences of ionic radii and chemical properties between 4f and 5f elements in separation chemistry. As far as we know, only three Am (III)-MOFs have been reported up to now.[10] Their results indicated many unexplored areas for Am-based structures, such as study of the covalency and the difference between $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ and trivalent lanthanide in the MOF system.
Phosphine oxides are highly stable ligands which have strong extraction ability but a moderate selectivity toward the trivalent actinides over lanthanides in the highly acidic nitrate solution.[11] Therefore, utilizing the organic ligand with phosphonyl group as linker to explore the difference between $\mathsf{A}\mathsf{m}^{3+}$ and lanthanide in MOFs system may be a feasible way. Herein, we report the synthesis methods, structural elucidation and UV–vis–NIR spectrum of a new $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ -based MOF, AmTPO ( ${\mathsf{T P O}}=$ tris(4-carboxylphenyl)- phosphineoxide), under mild solvothermal condition via using ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf T P O}_{.}$ , which has both of carboxyl and phosphonyl groups as organic linker. Furthermore, structural resistance data over several months is provided to illustrate the surprisingly high self-radiation resistance of transuranic MOFs.
## Results and Discussion
## Structural description
Single crystal X-ray diffraction revealed that AmTPO crystallizes in the centrosymmetric triclinic space group of $_{P-1}$ and detailed crystallography data are shown in Table S1. The crystal structure consists of one crystallographically distinct americium center, one TPO ligand unit, and one coordinated water molecule, as depicted in Figure 1a. The americium center forms an ellipsoidal polyhedron with nine coordinated oxygen, in which seven oxygen atoms come from carboxyl, one oxygen atom comes from $\mathsf{P}{=}\mathsf{O}$ group, one oxygen atom comes from coordinated water molecule. The eight $\mathsf{A m}{-}\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{L}}$ bond distances range from 2.285 Å to $2.448\mathrm{~\AA~},$ where all these oxygen atoms come from four TPO molecules. The ninth coordination site is occupied by a water molecule and the $\mathsf{A m}{-}\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{w}}$ bond distance is $2.470\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . However, direct comparison of selected trivalent metal–oxygen bond distances (Table S2) between compounds AmTPO and NdTPO is difficult owing to slight differences in asymmetric units.[12] Furthermore, two adjacent americium centers are connected by two bridging carboxyl group forming a pseudo-dimer building unit, as shown in Figure 1b. These dimer building units are then linked by the TPO ligands, forming an infinite layer along the bc plane (Figure 1c). Adjacent layers are connected with each other via TPO molecules forming a three-dimensional structure (Figure 1d). The bond valence sum (BVS, Table S3) values calculated for the americium ions is 3.262 valence units (v.u.), consistent with the trivalent assignment of Am in AmTPO.[13]
## Absorption spectroscopy
Solid state UV-vis-NIR spectroscopic data were collected on a single crystal of AmTPO ranging from 300 to 1200 nm at room temperature. As seen in Figure 1e, the spectrum of AmTPO clearly shows many characteristic f-f transitions associated with $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ For instance, the prominent peaks were observed at $507~\mathsf{n m}$ , 817 nm, and $1042\ \mathsf{n m}$ . The major peak around 507 nm can be attributed to the electron transition from 7F0 to 5L6.[14] The peak around $817~\mathsf{n m}$ corresponds to the electronic transition from $^7\mathsf{F}_{0}$ to $^7\mathsf{F}_{6}$ transitions. Furthermore, the lowest energy transition centered around 1042 nm is the electron transition from 7F0 to 7F4.[15]
## Radiation stability
Self-irradiation by higher-energy $\mathfrak{a}$ -particles significantly impacts the long-term stability of solid-state compounds bearing the radioactive nuclear element. Structure damage from self-irradiation is certainly unsurprising, and very common in the material with high-activity actinides. However, the self-irradiation effect in metal organic frameworks with high-activity actinides is rarely reported. To our knowledge, only a study about the $^{243}{\mathsf{A m-M O F}}$ was reported. The self-irradiation effect on the MOF crystallinity was monitored via its unit cell parameters.[10a]
AmTPO provided an opportunity to explore structural integrity of a rigid framework containing transuranic element. We studied the structure decay of one single crystal AmTPO over the course of several months since being synthesized and monitored its unit cell dimensions and cell volume. For AmTPO, an expansion of the unit cell volume by approximately $2\%$ was observed between one month and two months, then followed by a slight decrease of volume over the next month. This behavior is similar to the Am-GWMOF-6 (Figure 2a).[10a] On the contrary, Am-GWMOF-6 is assembled by $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ ions with adipic acid, in which the average bond energy is lower than that of conjugated π-systems in AmTPO. This results in more weaker radiation resistance of Am-GWMOF-6 compared to AmTPO. The structure of AmTPO is stable for longer than 120 d with self-radiation, however, the structure of Am-GWMOF-6 collapsed at 102 d after being prepared. Different from AmTPO, the cell volume expansion of $\mathsf{A m}|\mathsf{O}_{3}$ is slightly increased which may be a result of denser structure for AmIO3.[7] On the contrary, the TPO molecule may be rotated under the irradiation that leads to the cell expansion of MOFs. Furthermore, the unit cell parameters nearly unchanged over the course of two months since being synthesized (Table S4), demonstrating a continuously excellent crystallinity over time. As shown in Figure $2\mathsf{b},$ the main changing of cell parameter of AmTPO occurs at the bond angle of β. By analyzing the structure features of AmTPO, we suggested a stronger $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction and anti-irradiation ability of [100] and [001] planes over [010] plane. As the variation of unit cell parameters informed, we speculated the energy generated by self-irradiation may be mainly consumed via the rotation of β (Figures S1—S3) to maintain the structural integrity of AmTPO after irradiation. Consequently, structural damage of AmTPO mainly originates from the irradiation of $a-$ and $\gamma$ -rays as the result of the decay of $^{243}{\mathsf{A m}}$ . On the other hand, we also traced the changing of absorption spectrum for AmTPO. As shown in Figure 3, obvious changing of absorbance peak was observed at 300—450 nm, indicating the weaker coordination between $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ and TPO over the several months irradiation. Therefore, for excellent stability of AmTPO, extended $\pi$ -systems for organic linker is a necessary factor. Furthermore, the energy generated by self-irradiation effect could be weakened by the lattice vibration in the framework, which is another critical factor for its stability. The reasons for their excellent stability still need much more research to prove. However, strong radioactivity and high toxicity of transplutonium elements significantly limit the related research. We will further verify our hypothesis through a large number of experiments on other structures containing transplutonium elements.
## Conclusions
A new transplutonium $(\mathsf{A}\mathsf{m}^{3+})$ organic framework, named AmTPO, was synthesized under mild solvothermal condition and its structure was determined via single-crystal X-ray diffraction. For AmTPO, it contains americium dimer as the secondary building unit, fist-time seen in Am-MOF system. Furthermore, the UV-vis NIR absorption spectrum of AmTPO clearly shows the absorption peaks at 507 and 817 nm, the fingerprint peaks of Am3+ compounds. Moreover, AmTPO shows an excellent stability over the course of several months against self-irradiation. Both of extended π-systems for organic linker and weakening ray energy by the lattice vibration may be essential factors for excellent stability for AmTPO. This compound deepens our understanding of coordination mode of transplutonium elements ${\binom{243}{A\mathsf{m}}}\mathsf{A}$ in MOF structures and provides new hints for long-term disposal of americium, especially the design of its waste forms.
## Experimental
## Reagents and materials
Caution! Americium-243 exhibits significant combined radioand chemo-toxicity and represents serious health risks when inhaled or digested. All americium materials have to be handled either in radiological fume hoods or glove boxes and high-level precautions and procedures for handling radioactive materials must be followed. All americium experimental studies were conducted in a licensed laboratory dedicated to radiological investigations with approved safety operating procedures.
Preparation of $A m^{3+}$ stock solution. The stock solution of $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ was prepared by repeatedly dissolving ${\mathsf{A m}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ powder in concentrated nitric acid. $\mathsf{A m}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ was obtained via drying by distillation. Then water was added to prepared the solution of 0.1 $\mathsf{m o l/L A m(N O_{3})_{3}}$ .
Synthesis of AmTPO. A mixture of $\mathsf{A m}^{3+}$ solution $(50~\upmu\updownarrow,0.1$ $\mathsf{m o l}/\lfloor\mathsf{L}\rfloor$ , tris(4-carboxylphenyl)phosphineoxide $\left(0.00205\textrm{g,}0.005\right.$ mmol), tetrahydrofuran $(200\upmu\up L)$ , $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylformamide $(200~\upmu\up L)$ and deionized water $(200~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was loaded into a $10~\mathsf{m L}$ vial. The vial was sealed and heated at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 d and then cooled to room temperature. Pink block like crystals of AmTPO were deposited. All reagents and solvents employed were commercially available and used as received.
## Characterization
X-ray crystallography studies. Single crystal X-ray diffraction data collection of AmTPO was performed on a Bruker D8-Venture diffractometer with a Turbo X-ray Source (Mo- $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{a}$ radiation, $\lambda=$ 0.71073 Å) adopting the direct-drive rotating anode technique and a CMOS detector under $298\mathsf{K}.$ The data of AmTPO were collected using the program APEX3 and then processed using SAINT routine in APEX3. The structure of AmTPO was solved by direct methods and refined by the full-matrix least squares on $\Dot{F}^{2}$ using the SHELXTL.
UV/Vis spectra analysis. The solid-state UV–vis–NIR absorption spectrum data was measured on a single crystal of AmTPO from 300 to $1200\ \mathsf{n m}$ using a Craic Technologies micro-spectrophotometer.
